 17592186045417] [17592186050585 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050585 #edb/ref 1031 "Rectangles, Rhombuses, and Squares" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050585 #edb/ref 1033 3751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050585 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "55c78688-c952-5199-8043-1a8c75fa6d8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051086 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051086 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051086 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051086 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051086 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186142939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051086 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186142940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051086 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186142941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051086 #edb/ref 1031 "Quadrilaterals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051086 #edb/ref 1033 2407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051086 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7ffc94a4-f7fc-52b7-845d-79695ef0c9c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073598 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073598 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the two polygons shown below are similar, determine the similarity ratio.\n\n*Hint: Consider the ratio of the **larger** polygon to the **smaller** polygon.*\n\n![](images/f4/f4da94c11054e9e42b7dc9f6c085d3c6be5fdf026ba9d913fb7b060d56221967.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073598 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da7af116-26c8-56e4-a6e5-b195cbb7699e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073598 #edb/ref 1074 "Given two similar polygons, the similarity ratio is the ratio of any two corresponding sides. \n\nNotice that, for example, $\\overline{LM}$ is the longest side of $NLMP.$ Hence, it corresponds to the longest side of $ABCD,$ which is  $\\overline{BC}.$ \n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{BC}{LM} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The ratio of the smaller polygon to the larger polygon would be $\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073599 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2969" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073599 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following quadrilaterals are similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ (other than $\\mathcal R$ itself)? \n\n![](images/5a/5a7c9208a16a639ef6b08855a6caaacb39c121dd4ccf45271d98fcc70f1a8fed.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd7f22e7-9aba-5936-beb1-56d3a5e016cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073599 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $\\mathcal{R}$ is a rectangle, meaning all the angles measure $90^\\circ,$ and it has $2$ pairs of parallel congruent sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $\\mathcal{Q}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}.$ The polygon $\\mathcal{Q}$ is a rectangle, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{3}{3}\n$$\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is similar to $\\mathcal{R}.$ Indeed, $\\mathcal{P}$ is a rectangle. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n- $\\mathcal{S}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ because $\\mathcal{S}$ is not a rectangle (it's a parallelogram).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{P}$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073600 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2970" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073600 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the two quadrilaterals below are similar, write down a correct similarity statement.\n\n![](images/bf/bfff4195302736df01985ca9db83d373243833023003730004cd93512467e4f4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073600 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98596871-dc8e-5083-a773-1a70ffe1a159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073600 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a quadrilateral is $360^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {55}^{∘}+m∠B+{90}^{∘}+{125}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B+{270}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠K+m∠L+m∠M+m∠N & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {55}^{∘}+{90}^{∘}+m∠M+{125}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠M+{270}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠M & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the quadrilaterals are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠A≅∠K,\\,∠D≅∠N,\\,∠C≅∠L≅∠B≅∠M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo far, we have that \n\n- $A$ corresponds to $K,$ and\n\n- $D$ corresponds to $N.$\n\nFrom the angle measurements, it's unclear whether $C$ corresponds to $L$ or $M$ (these are all right angles). However, $C$ sits between a $125^\\circ$ angle and a $90^\\circ$ angle, and so does $M$ (whereas $L$ does not). So, we also have that \n\n- $C$ corresponds to $M,$ and\n\n- $B$ corresponds to $L.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct similarity statement is \n\n$$\nABCD \\sim  KLMN.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073601 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073601 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $ABCD \\sim A'B'C'D',$ $AB = 3\\,\\mathrm{ft},$ and $BC = 4\\,\\mathrm{ft},$\nwhich of the following could be the values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'?$\n\nI. ${A'B'} = 15\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 20\\, \\mathrm{ft}$\n\nII. ${A'B'} = 30\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 44\\, \\mathrm{ft}$\n\nIII. ${A'B'} = 18\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 24\\, \\mathrm{ft}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073601 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fda1604-bea3-5061-9a3f-a0ec86f95a41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073601 #edb/ref 1074 "For similar polygons, the ratios of lengths of the corresponding sides should be the same.\n\n- For option I, the ratios are the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{3}{15}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{20}=\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option I indeed gives possible values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'.$\n\n- For option II, the ratios are different:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{3}{30}=\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{44}=\\frac{1}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option II does not give possible values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'.$\n\n- For option III, the ratios are the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{3}{18}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{24}=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III indeed gives possible values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073912 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073912 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073912 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073912 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Similarity Ratio Between Two Similar Polygons" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073912 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073912 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6447abce-804f-5569-90ef-32d989cc7a06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073913 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073913 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073913 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073913 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516822 #edb/ref 175921