 [17592186516729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516729 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516729 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516729 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516729 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516729 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a2d05394-8b11-58dd-b0a5-7d79939aa2f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516745 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516745 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516745 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516745 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516745 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d26a1b39-5d9a-522e-b93e-64f3b7de831a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516746 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516746 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516746 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516746 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d2b35f2c-efc3-59cb-b3ac-4a86eb509d98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516761 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516761 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516761 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516761 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516761 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516761 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516761 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516761 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516761 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f127219a-5ce9-57cb-92d2-db82d4cf9049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516775 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516775 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516775 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516775 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $ABCD∼LMNO,$ $CD=4\\,m$ and $AD=1\\,m,$\nwhich of the following could be the values of $NO$ and $LO?$\n\nI. $NO=12\\,m,\\,LO=3\\,m$\n\nII. $NO=16\\,m,\\,LO=5\\,m$\n\nIII. $NO=10\\,m,\\,LO=2\\,m$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516775 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11d089d5-e1bc-5fc5-afc2-069d5d90d5e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516775 #edb/ref 1074 "For similar polygons, the ratios of lengths of the corresponding sides should be the same.\n\n- Let's check for this in option I:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{CD}{NO} & =\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\frac{AD}{LO} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option I gives possible values of $NO$ and $LO.$\n\n- For option II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{CD}{NO} & =\\frac{4}{16}=\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\frac{AD}{LO} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III does *not* give possible values of $NO$ and $LO.$\n\n- For option III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{CD}{NO} & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\frac{AD}{LO} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III does *not* give possible values of $NO$ and $LO.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516786 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516786 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516786 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516786 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e7/e7c6cb8f0d7746c1d923ed80a9e05ba4f8eeb9ea3ae88c7b08bcd02037d8ec8a.png)\n\nThe two triangles shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516786 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20014b11-37e4-5374-8b1b-750de6a641aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516786 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a triangle is ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠H+m∠J+m∠L & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H+{69}^{∘}+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H+{109}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H & ={71}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠V+m∠W+m∠Y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {71}^{∘}+m∠W+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠W+{111}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠W & ={69}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the triangles are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠H≅∠V,\\,∠J≅∠W,\\,∠L≅∠Y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n- $H$ corresponds to $V,$\n\n- $J$ corresponds to $W,$\n\n- $L$ corresponds to $Y.$\n\nTherefore, $HJL∼VWY.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516792 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516792 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516792 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516792 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/83/83881bdbd7df1527338db3de5e88b69003b7d30c8c25d4583127b1579a28cf83.png)\n\nGiven that $△DEF∼△PQR$ (picture is not to scale), find the similarity ratio.\n\n*Hint: Consider the ratio of the **larger** polygon to the **smaller** polygon.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516792 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3223d2f4-ea53-55be-b26b-e4775a16b327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516792 #edb/ref 1074 "The similarity ratio is the ratio of the corresponding sides.\n\nSince $△DEF∼△PQR,$ the side $\\overset{―}{DE}$ corresponds to the side $\\overset{―}{PQ}$\n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{PQ}{DE} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516812 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516812 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516812 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516812 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/392309f189b8840862795d3b111c20956ec2366b349c6a60a6b0288052d451c2.png)\n\nGiven that $△ABC∼△VTU$ (picture is not to scale), find the similarity ratio.\n\n*Hint: Consider the ratio of the **smaller** polygon to the **larger** polygon.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516812 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c6e7fd6-6ef2-5487-99ae-05f731828948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516812 #edb/ref 1074 "The similarity ratio is the ratio of the corresponding sides.\n\nSince $△ABC∼△VTU,$ the side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ corresponds to the side $\\overset{―}{TU}$\n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{BC}{TU} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516815 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516815 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516815 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516815 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8f/8fca4f1d297c465ecf9da9bef652f1602edb6e6cd8887f6b3b8a83441a40871b.png)\n\nThe two quadrilaterals shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516815 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7546430f-d626-5e00-b8d1-dad9719eee9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516815 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a quadrilateral is ${360}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠R+m∠T+m∠U+m∠V & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠R+{130}^{∘}+{50}^{∘}+{110}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠R+{290}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠R & ={70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠O+m∠N+m∠Q+m∠P & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {70}^{∘}+{130}^{∘}+m∠Q+{110}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠Q+{310}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠Q & ={50}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the quadrilaterals are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠R≅∠O,\\,∠T≅∠N,\\,∠U≅∠Q,\\,∠V≅∠P.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n- $R$ corresponds to $O,$\n\n- $T$ corresponds to $N,$\n\n- $U$ corresponds to $Q,$\n\n- $V$ corresponds to $P.$\n\nTherefore, $RTUV∼ONQP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516822 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516822 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516822 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516822 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e4/e4a79a8b0eb2c0c79b1cdcb0623dc913169db8a46aab9b0a18c23f96ac61a0cd.png)\n\nWhich of the above quadrilaterals are similar to $P$ (other than $P$ itself)?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516822 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8747f1cc-7c01-5370-9ad7-927463f6175f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516822 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $P$ is a kite with a pair of opposite congruent angles and $2$ pairs of congruent sides. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $R$ is *not* similar to $P$ because $R$ is not a kite.\n\n- $S$ is similar to $P.$ Indeed, $S$ is a kite. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2.5}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\n- $T$ is similar to $P.$ Indeed, $T$ is a kite. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2.5}{2.5}=\\frac{5}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ and $T$ only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516824 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516824 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516824 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516824 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1a/1ad27414fe6da6009a54815aff3137365067218e5b73f62e34ef6d3ae2527058.png)\n\nWhich of the above quadrilaterals are similar to $T$ (other than $T$ itself)?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516824 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c8cab05-4b0d-578a-9cd0-38ffa882719a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516824 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $T$ is a trapezoid with one pair of parallel opposite sides. Since the angles adjacent to the bases are congruent, it is an isosceles trapezoid. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $S$ is *not* similar to $T.$ The polygon $S$ is an isosceles trapezoid, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same: \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{16}\\ne \\frac{4}{4}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* similar to $T$ because $Q$ is a right trapezoid since it has right angles.\n\n- $P$ is similar to $T.$ Indeed, $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{16}=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
